\details{
\strong{Method.} The fit uses \link[=design_qr]{qr()} on the (weighted) design
matrix: \code{R} is the Cholesky factor of \verb{X'WX}, computed from factor codes
and numeric variables; columns are pivoted, and aliased columns detected,
as in \code{\link[=lm]{lm()}} (same tolerance, same \code{NA} pattern in the coefficients); the
coefficients are refined from the residuals. Results agree with those of
\code{lm()} up to floating point rounding.

\strong{Compression.} When many rows share a design pattern (the same factor
levels and numeric values in every variable of the design), the fit only
needs, for each pattern, the sum of the weights, the weighted mean of the
response (minus the offset) and the weighted sum of squares around that
mean. The coefficients, residual sum of squares and covariance matrix
computed from these sufficient statistics are those of the fit on every
row, so a fit on millions of rows of categorical data costs about as
much as one on its distinct patterns. Patterns are found by the
deduplication machinery of the products (see \link{design_algebra} and the
option \code{design.matrix.dedup}). With \code{compress = "auto"}, the fit is
compressed when at most half the rows are distinct patterns, and the
search for patterns gives up as soon as that is exceeded; the search is
skipped when the option \code{design.matrix.dedup} is \code{FALSE}. \code{compress = TRUE} always compresses (unless every row is distinct) and \code{FALSE} never
does.

\strong{What is kept.} The fit keeps the coefficients, the factor \code{R}, the
residual and explained sums of squares, and the design specification,
which \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}} applies to \code{newdata} with \code{design_matrix(spec, newdata)}. Per-row residuals and fitted values are not stored: with
\code{model = TRUE} (the default, as in \code{\link[=lm]{lm()}}) the fit keeps the model frame
and its design matrix, which share memory with the frame, and
\code{\link[=residuals]{residuals()}}, \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} and \code{predict()} without \code{newdata} recompute
them (one product with the design). With \code{model = FALSE} these need
\code{newdata}.

\strong{Methods.} \code{\link[=coef]{coef()}}, \code{\link[=vcov]{vcov()}} (with \code{NA} rows and columns for aliased
coefficients, as \code{vcov(lm_fit)}), \code{\link[=sigma]{sigma()}}, \code{\link[=df.residual]{df.residual()}}, \code{\link[=nobs]{nobs()}},
\code{\link[=deviance]{deviance()}} (the weighted residual sum of squares), \code{\link[=logLik]{logLik()}} (hence
\code{\link[=AIC]{AIC()}} and \code{\link[=BIC]{BIC()}}), \code{\link[=formula]{formula()}}, \code{\link[=terms]{terms()}}, \code{\link[=model.frame]{model.frame()}},
