\section{Fixed effects}{

A formula \code{y ~ x | fe1 + fe2} absorbs fixed effects, as
\code{\link[fixest:feols]{fixest::feols()}}: each FE is a variable (a factor, character, integer or
numeric column) or an interaction \code{a^b}. The fit never builds the FE
dummies or demeans the rows: it solves the normal equations from sums
over rows (the design's products, sums within FE levels and crosstabs of
the FE), eliminating the largest FE exactly and solving for the others
densely (up to 3000 levels) or by conjugate gradients with an incomplete
Cholesky preconditioner. The same code fits data in memory and remote
data (DuckDB tables or queries, Parquet), with identical results up to
rounding.
\itemize{
\item Rows: rows with a missing value in the model, FE or cluster variables
are dropped, as are rows with zero weight and, with \code{fixef.rm = "singleton"} (default), singletons (rows alone in a level of some FE),
iteratively, as \code{feols()} does. \code{fit$nsingletons} counts them.
\item Coefficients: the intercept is absorbed; a column collinear with the
FE or earlier columns is \code{NA} (fixest's rule: its residual variance is
below \code{tol} times its variance; \code{tol} defaults to \code{1e-10} with FE, as
\code{collin.tol}). One step of iterative refinement from the residuals is
taken, and numeric variables far from zero are centered when that is
an exact transform of the design, so that coefficients and standard
errors stay accurate.
\item Standard errors: \code{vcov = "iid"} (default) or \code{vcov = ~cl} (clustered by
one variable), with fixest's default small sample corrections
(\code{ssc(K.adj = TRUE, K.fixef = "nonnested", G.adj = TRUE)}); clustered
tests in \code{summary()} use \code{t(G - 1)}. The cluster scores are corrected
for FE not nested in the clusters. \code{fit$fe_dof} is the number of FE
parameters counted (\verb{sum(levels) - (number of FE - 1)}); \code{fit$fe_dof_exact}
the exact rank of the FE (fixest's \code{K.exact = TRUE}), exact for one or
two FE and for three or more when the reduced system was factored
(otherwise \code{NA}).
\item Methods: those of a \code{design_lm} fit (class \code{c("design_lm_fe", "design_lm")}); \code{summary()} reports R-squared and within R-squared.
\code{fixef()} (the generic of fixest or nlme, when loaded) returns the FE
values, also in \code{fit$fixef}: one named vector per FE, normalized with
one level per connected set of levels at zero in every FE but the
largest (differences within a connected set are those of fixest).
\code{predict(fit, newdata)} is \verb{X b} plus the FE values of the rows' levels
(\code{NA} for levels not in the fit). With remote data, \code{fitted()} and
\code{predict()} return tables and \code{residuals()} a remote design, as for
remote fits without FE.
\item Not supported with FE: \code{subset}, offsets, \code{compress = TRUE}, \code{se.fit}
and intervals in \code{predict()}, clustering by more than one variable.
For remote data, the response and \code{weights} are column names,
\code{DBI::SQL()} or R expressions, as without FE (expressions with no SQL
translation are evaluated in R, in batches).
\item Options: \code{design.matrix.fe_solver} (\code{"auto"}, \code{"dense"}, \code{"pcg"}, or
\code{"chol"} for a sparse Cholesky factor), \code{design.matrix.fe_precond}
(\code{"jacobi"}, default, or \code{"ichol"} for an incomplete Cholesky factor),
\code{design.matrix.fe_refine} (refinement steps,
default 1) and \code{design.matrix.fe_center} (default \code{TRUE}).
}
}

\examples{
## fixed effects (examples below: without)
set.seed(2)
panel <- data.frame(id = rep(1:100, each = 10), t = rep(1:10, 100), x = rnorm(1000))
panel$y <- panel$x + panel$id / 50 + rnorm(1000)
fe <- design_lm(y ~ x | id + t, panel, vcov = ~id)
summary(fe)
head(fe$fixef$id)

set.seed(1)
d <- data.frame(y = rnorm(1000), x = rnorm(1000),
                f = factor(sample(letters[1:5], 1000, TRUE)),
                g = factor(sample(1:3, 1000, TRUE)))
fit <- design_lm(y ~ x * f, d)
all.equal(coef(fit), coef(lm(y ~ x * f, d)))
all.equal(vcov(fit), vcov(lm(y ~ x * f, d)))
summary(fit)

## factors only: fitted on 15 patterns instead of 1000 rows
fit <- design_lm(y ~ f * g, d)
fit$patterns
all.equal(sigma(fit), sigma(lm(y ~ f * g, d)))

predict(fit, newdata = d[1:3, ])
}
